    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let’s analyze the recurrence $x_n x_{n+1} = n$. Then $\\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = \\frac{n}{x_{n+1}^2}$. Given $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = 1$, so $x_{n+1}^2 \\sim n$, i.e., $x_n \\sim \\sqrt{n}$. Using Wallis product: $\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1} \\frac{(2k)(2k)}{(2k-1)(2k+1)} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2n}{2n+1}$. From recurrence, $x_1^2 = \\frac{1}{x_2^2} = \\frac{1}{1/x_1^2} = x_1^2$, but better: compute $x_1^2 = \\frac{1}{x_2^2} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x_1^2}}$ — not helpful. Instead, observe $x_2 = \\frac{1}{x_1}$, $x_3 = \\frac{2}{x_2} = 2x_1$, $x_4 = \\frac{3}{x_3} = \\frac{3}{2x_1}$, $x_5 = \\frac{4}{x_4} = \\frac{8x_1}{3}$, etc. So $x_{2n} = \\frac{(2n-1)!!}{(2n-2)!!} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x_1}$, $x_{2n+1} = \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n-1)!!} \\cdot x_1$. As $n\\to\\infty$, ratio $x_n / x_{n+1} \\to 1$ implies $\\frac{x_{2n}}{x_{2n+1}} \\to 1$, so $\\frac{(2n-1)!!^2}{(2n-2)!! (2n)!!} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x_1^2} \\to 1$. Using Wallis: $\\frac{(2n)!!^2}{(2n-1)!! (2n+1)!!} \\to \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $\\frac{(2n-1)!!^2}{(2n-2)!! (2n)!!} \\to \\frac{2}{\\pi}$. Hence $\\frac{2}{\\pi} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x_1^2} = 1 \\Rightarrow x_1^2 = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\Rightarrow \\pi x_1^2 = 2$.\n\n$\\boxed{B}$",
