ns total energy, and $ \\phi = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 $ implies $ dW = \\phi di = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 di $, integrate from i=0 to i=2 sin(1): $ W = \\frac{1}{12} i^4 \\big|_0^{2\\sin 1} = \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 16 \\sin^4 1 = \\frac{4}{3} \\sin^4 1 \\approx \\frac{4}{3}(0.5) = \\frac{2}{3} $. Not in options. Alternatively, maybe they meant energy at t=1: $ W = \\int_0^{i(1)} \\phi di = \\frac{1}{12} (2\\sin 1)^4 \\approx 0.67 $. Closest to 0.75. Or perhaps misinterpretation: $ \\phi $ is flux linkage, and energy is $ \\frac{1}{2} \\phi i $ only for linear? No. Wait! For any inductor, $ W = \\int_0^i \\phi(i') di' $. So yes, $ \\int_0^{2\\sin 1} \\frac{1}{3}i'^3 di' = \\frac{1}{12}(2\\sin 1)^4 $. Compute numerically: sin(1) ≈ 0.8415, sin^4(1) ≈ 0.500, so (2)^4 * 0.500 = 16*0.5=8, 8/12=0.666… ≈ 0.75. So B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
